<article class="env-remark" id="005S">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/005S">Remark <span data-tag="005S">5.17.6</span>.</a> Here are some references to the literature. In <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">I, p. 75, Theorem 1</span>, <a href="/bibliography/Bourbaki">Bourbaki</a>]</span> you can find: (2) $\Leftrightarrow $ (4). In <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">I, p. 77, Proposition 6</span>, <a href="/bibliography/Bourbaki">Bourbaki</a>]</span> you can find: (2) $\Rightarrow $ (1). Of course, trivially we have (1) $\Rightarrow $ (4). Thus (1), (2) and (4) are equivalent. The equivalence of (3) and (4) is <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Chapter II, Exercise 25</span>, <a href="/bibliography/LangReal">LangReal</a>]</span>. </p>
